WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.469
Film Courage: I believe you made your first
feature film in 2013?

00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:05.960
Did you think upon making this that your life
was going to change?

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.130
Aaron Fradkin, Writer/Director/Youtuber: I
guess if I were to ever have a period of my

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:17.289
life where I did have a chip on my shoulder
it was in 2012 making that movie.

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:21.710
I didn't really know what was gonna happen
with it.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:28.630
I was very much focused on just making a movie
that I wanted to make and had zero intention

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:32.029
of trying to make something marketable or
anything.

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:38.840
All I wanted out of it was sort of just to
open a door and have maybe an agent that can

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:45.610
help me make my next movie or garner the attention
of some investors that would finance the next

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.000
movie.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.750
While making it was an absolute blast and
I definitely hope for the best I didn't if

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.540
you were to actually ask me truthfully if
I thought this was going to be my big break.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:02.170
I'd probably say no I don't think so but I
just sort of wanted to prove to myself that

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:08.260
I could make a coherent story and I think
I successfully did that at least you know

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.800
when I was 20-23 years old or so.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.940
Film Courage: Do you really think that's a
chip on your shoulder or more just creative

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.150
longing, just wanting to make something?

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:22.160
Aaron: It was probably more creative longing.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.860
I mean it wasn't like in a cocky way.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:30.790
I of course hoped that we would get in a Sundance
and make a million dollars and there was a

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:36.340
small part of my mind that thought that could
happen i could see that happening now i don't

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:42.060
really think in that way so so when I was
younger I definitely you know the whole Sundance

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:49.000
dream and selling your movie for millions
and millions of dollars was it was much more

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:56.020
of a possibility in my mind back then whereas
now it might happen but I don't even think

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.020
about that.

00:01:57.020 --> 00:02:02.780
Now I focus so much more on the actual content
I'm trying to make and just making the best

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.870
possible movie I can.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:10.920
Film Courage: Would you advise other first-time
filmmakers not just a short but a feature

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.500
that would be really more of a again cliche
term but a passion project rather than aiming

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.370
for this incredible distribution deal?

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.150
Aaron: That's a very difficult question to
answer and I know.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.280
I'm sure a lot of people will watch this and
and I want to be careful with my word choice

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.819
because I don't want to like lead anyone down
the wrong path.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:41.060
Obviously everyone's different and personally
I think it's important to think you know about

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:46.730
the business of it and also think creatively
just how everything else seems to be a balance

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:52.480
in life you also need the balance of business
and creative like you at least if you want

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:59.880
to make a living doing this and only this
personally I think that's really important.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:06.319
There are plenty of people who I look up to
that not once have ever thought about the

00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.459
business part of things in fact they despise
it and they've only focused on the creative

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:15.290
part of it and and that's worked out well
for them and then there's plenty of people

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.390
that only focus on the business and you watch
their movie and you can tell but the business

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.920
is really working out and the creativity part
of it kind of suffers.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.360
For me I like to keep both in mind all the
time when I'm actually on set or when I'm

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.680
actually in front of my computer writing.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:38.850
I first and foremost I do my best to compartmentalize
and I just focus on the story and what it

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:44.040
is that I'm actually writing about what it
is I want to say and then when we go through

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.950
the revision stage it's like all right well
this might be more relatable or we might be

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:53.680
able to actually call this a horror film if
we add a few more you know jump scares or

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.930
you know just sort of add to it fluff it up
to sort of take your original creative vision

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:03.680
and make it a little bit more marketable so
you can make money and do it again.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:10.250
Film Courage: What mistakes did you make with
15 North that have always stuck with you that

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:16.030
then going forward with a new project you
always remember those mistakes and try to

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:20.519
correct anything whether it's in pre, production,
post?

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:25.819
Aaron: I think I spent too much on post-production.

00:04:25.819 --> 00:04:33.309
I realized later on that I could actually
do a lot more myself at least in that small

00:04:33.309 --> 00:04:39.870
of a budget I could do a lot more instead
of spending money and hiring you know someone

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:46.039
else to do certain things although then again
the entire landscape has changed and you're

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:51.199
able to do so much more from home now than
you were just seven years ago.

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:56.569
I'm trying to think of of the biggest mistake?

00:04:56.569 --> 00:05:03.330
I would say the biggest mistake that I made
on my first movie was not spending enough

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:11.550
time on the script i definitely approached
the entire project with this attitude of I

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:16.839
just want to make a movie I want to make it
real I'm just going to keep the cameras rolling

00:05:16.839 --> 00:05:23.050
and you know get natural performances it's
very sort of mumble core style and I think

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.360
that that is good but I didn't prepare enough.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:33.930
I feel like I could have fleshed out the story
a lot more with you know just really focus

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:41.639
on a more traditional three-act structure
and focus on writing characters with more

00:05:41.639 --> 00:05:47.169
depth and and that sort of thing if there
was one thing I could do over again that would

00:05:47.169 --> 00:05:51.000
probably be it is just spend more time focusing
on the actual script.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.960
Film Courage: Do you think that prompted your
Youtube channel because you give such helpful

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.289
tips with your channel do you think that prompted
you to want to start it or no?

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:09.399
Aaron: CertainlyI think the content I'm putting
out on on my Youtube channels is basically

00:06:09.399 --> 00:06:13.139
just everything I wanted to see.

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:19.969
When I was a few years younger I wanted to
see people creating content from home by themselves

00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:26.990
and I enjoy that I love seeing people and
what they're able to do without big studios

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:28.569
behind them.

00:06:28.569 --> 00:06:29.569
Would I take a studio job?

00:06:29.569 --> 00:06:38.539
Of course but I really get a kick out of this
just home brewed you know do-it-yourself kind

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:39.780
of approach.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:48.650
Film Courage: I think your top video is about
using the drawing aspect in Adobe?

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:52.529
Aaron: Oh yeah, the scribble animation, that's
funny.

00:06:52.529 --> 00:06:55.629
Film Courage: Did you know that would be so
popular?

00:06:55.629 --> 00:06:57.819
Aaron: No not really.

00:06:57.819 --> 00:07:05.559
I mean I know it's a popular effect but yeah
with that kind of thing you never really know

00:07:05.559 --> 00:07:07.509
it's gonna be popular.

00:07:07.509 --> 00:07:11.610
With our other channel Social House Films
that's a lot more cut and dry.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:18.020
If you make a short horror film and it looks
pretty and the monster looks scary then it's

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:20.559
probably gonna do pretty well.

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:27.419
These tutorials it's hard to sort of be on
the pulse of what effect is going to be.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:34.139
The most desirable now that now that TikTok
is a thing you know a lot of people are trying

00:07:34.139 --> 00:07:39.089
to like create their own effects at home and
and so I never know what that next thing is

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:40.089
going to be.

00:07:40.089 --> 00:07:40.104
Question for the Viewers: What was the biggest
mistake you made on your first movie?

